  "tour": {
    "title": "The Divine Council: From the Old Testament to the New",
    "intro": "This tour starts with the basic biblical picture of God’s heavenly administration, then follows that picture through the Old Testament’s stories of the nations, Babel, and Psalm 82. From there, you’ll hear how Jesus and Paul assume this world—and how Hebrews reimagines God’s heavenly assembly around Jesus and the new covenant.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:theme-god-e3-spiritual-warfare",
        "startSec": 385,
        "endSec": 685,
        "why": "Begin here for the basic vocabulary and image. The teachers define the divine council as God’s “commander-in-chief” relationship to a staff of other spiritual beings, then walk through 1 Kings 22, where Yahweh sits on his throne with “all the host of heaven” around him. The clip also explains why “Elohim” can refer broadly to spiritual beings without putting them on Yahweh’s level.",
        "episodeTitle": "Spiritual Warfare",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/b40c2f6a-4f0a-4552-a28b-ad3991133387/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=b40c2f6a-4f0a-4552-a28b-ad3991133387",
        "durationSec": 4363,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:2563",
        "startSec": 800,
        "endSec": 1100,
        "why": "This supplies the Old Testament’s larger storyline. The speaker connects Exodus 19’s “kingdom of priests” and “treasured possession” language to Deuteronomy 32, where the nations are allotted to “the sons of God” and Israel becomes Yahweh’s inheritance. Listen for the claim that the nations’ spiritual rulers were meant to be caretakers, but that Psalm 82 presents them as corrupt—an interpretive framework for Babel, Israel, and the nations.",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 286: Exodus 19",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/NakedBible286_Exodus19.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3293,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:ee2d4ff5-com-6e99511a-003e-300d-be0a-42d127b215f3",
        "startSec": 740,
        "endSec": 1040,
        "why": "Now hear the Old Testament worldview operating inside a New Testament argument. In John 10, Jesus quotes Psalm 82’s “You are gods,” and this teacher notes the interpretive question—human judges or supernatural members of the divine assembly—before arguing that the latter best fits the psalm’s scene. The clip shows how Jesus uses that scriptural category to defend his claim to be the uniquely sent “Son of God,” rather than retreating from the charge of blasphemy.",
        "episodeTitle": "John 10:22-42",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/y9bntfdedmsazpzr/john_1022-42929g5.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1399,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:paul-and-powers",
        "startSec": 740,
        "endSec": 1040,
        "why": "This is the bridge from the Old Testament’s gods and nations to Paul’s “principalities and powers.” N. T. Wright describes how Jesus confronts an unseen enemy and says that what appears in the Gospels is “what was always latent” in the Hebrew Scriptures. He also explains how idolatry gives evil forces “power over” humans and the world, while cautioning that language about spiritual hierarchies is necessarily approximate.",
        "episodeTitle": "N.T. Wright Interview: Paul and the Powers in Acts",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/c64d3f2d-90df-4f7a-8f94-cef76703d210/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=c64d3f2d-90df-4f7a-8f94-cef76703d210",
        "durationSec": 3835,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:2856",
        "startSec": 927,
        "endSec": 1200,
        "why": "End with the payoff for Christian reading and identity. Hebrews 12 is read as more than a metaphor about earlier believers cheering from the sidelines: “Mount Zion,” the heavenly Jerusalem, angels, the assembly of the firstborn, God, and Jesus form a divine-council-like gathering. The speaker emphasizes that believers “have come” to this assembly already, with Jesus as mediator of the new covenant—an “already, but of course not yet” way to read the New Testament’s participation in God’s heavenly world.",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 340: The Cloud of Witnesses in Hebrews 12",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/NakedBible340_CloudofWitnesses_Hebrews12.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3549,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips present the divine council not as a detached curiosity but as a biblical way of imagining God’s rule: Yahweh is supreme, heavenly beings are real but subordinate, the nations’ rebellion matters, and Jesus is the decisive Son and Lord who brings people into God’s assembly. As you keep reading, watch for thrones, heavenly hosts, sons of God, nations, powers, rulers, authorities, and assemblies—and ask how each passage places Jesus within that story.",
    "totalSeconds": 1473
  },
